Package: KimNIRA
Type: Package
Title: Kinetic Ising Model with NodeIdentifyR Algorithm for Dynamic Network
    Intervention Analysis
Version: 1.1.0
Authors@R: person("Fei", "Wang",
    email = "bjnwangfei0501@outlook.com",
    role  = c("aut", "cre"))
Description: Implements a complete analytical workflow for dynamic binary
    psychological network analysis by integrating the Kinetic Ising Model
    (KIM) with the NodeIdentifyR Algorithm (NIRA). The package covers six
    stages: (1) Network estimation — fits temporal (within-person) and
    between-subject networks to long-format binary ecological momentary
    assessment (EMA) data via LASSO-penalised pseudo-likelihood logistic
    regression; (2) Moderation testing — tests whether any node moderates
    temporal edge weights as a prerequisite for NIRA, using likelihood-ratio
    tests and permutation-based inference to verify the constant-edge-weight
    assumption; (3) Intervention simulation — runs the NodeIdentifyR Algorithm
    (NIRA) via paired Monte Carlo simulation of alleviating or aggravating
    interventions on each node, ranking targets by effect size; (4) Significance
    testing — applies paired sign-flip permutation tests with multiple-comparison
    correction across all intervention nodes; (5) Stability assessment — repeats
    NIRA across independent random seeds and summarises node-ranking stability
    via rank-proportion matrices and position-weighted consensus rankings;
    (6) Centrality analysis — computes and visualises InStrength, OutStrength,
    and signed Expected Influence indices for both the temporal and
    between-subject networks, correctly handling the column-to-row direction
    convention of the KIM temporal edge matrix.
    Visualisation functions cover temporal and between-subject network plots,
    NIRA mean projection plots with significance stars, longitudinal intervention
    trajectory plots (summary, node-level, difference, panel, and spaghetti
    variants), stability stacked-bar charts, a moderation heatmap, and
    centralityplots (via qgraph and ggplot2).
